oaxaca-installer (0.1.2) stonking; urgency=medium

  * Keep the previous system bootable on a shared EFI System Partition: a new
    shellprocess@loader step reads the /EFI/ubuntu/grub.cfg stub the bootloader
    step is about to overwrite and writes /etc/grub.d/45_oaxaca_previous in
    the target, a menu entry that loads that system's own grub.cfg; the
    bootloader no longer overwrites EFI/BOOT (installEFIFallback: false), and
    os-prober is on (Legion laptop, 2026-10-12: Kubuntu's loader overwritten).
  * mount.conf binds /run/systemd/resolve and /cdrom into the target, as
    Ubuntu's settings do; the welcome page checks internet without requiring
    it, the hardware scripts' packages being in the image now.
  * The GRUB menu entry is the distro's name: grubcfg no longer keeps Ubuntu's
    GRUB_DISTRIBUTOR shell expression, which Calamares wrote back quoted and
    unevaluated; the branding's bootloaderEntryName names the distro.
  * Record the created user as SDDM's last user (sddm-last-user, run from the
    live system against the target: the packages step removes this package
    from the target first), so the login screen, which has no user field,
    logs that user in on first boot.
  * keep-previous-loader reads the stub from the EFI System Partition the
    target's fstab names when /boot/efi is not mounted, and logs every
    decision to /var/log/oaxaca/keep-previous-loader.log in the target
    (an install on 2026-10-12 wrote no entry and left no reason).
  * Encryption by default: partition.conf sets preCheckEncryption, so the
    Encrypt box comes up checked for Erase disk and Replace a partition.
  * Branding: windowExpanding noexpand at 1100x720, so the Summary page scrolls
    inside the window instead of growing past it and hiding the Install button.
  * Record the previous system in /etc/oaxaca/previous-loader and run
    loader-fallback after the bootloader step (shellprocess@fallback), so the
    shared stub falls back to the previous system when this one is removed.

 -- santisbon <santisbon@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 13:00:00 -0500

oaxaca-installer (0.1.1) stonking; urgency=medium

  * Depend on rsync, btrfs-progs and xfsprogs: Calamares's unpackfs module
    copies the squashfs with rsync, which Ubuntu's calamares package does not
    depend on, so the first install on the Legion laptop failed with "rsync
    failed with error code 127" (2026-10-11); the partition page offers btrfs
    and xfs, whose mkfs tools were missing too.

